I would hate to be Brady Olson, without a doubt. This may be a small sample size, but even Williams saw a minuscule decrease from 2021 to 2022. You could attribute it to the change from the terracotta of Oklahoma to the sandy beaches of Los Angeles, but Lincoln Riley went with him. His efficiency dropped a mere 1.1 points. But everyone else? Various amounts of decline. And then there is Brady Olson.
The big one to look at here is Williams, who is the primary then-Power Five constant. Henigan is also a good case study for the 2021 class, posting a very flat dip as well, playing at a perennial then-G5 power and annual bowl contender in Memphis. The backbones at the other schools are, well, to be frank, meh.
What about the money-maker? Touchdowns is a driving factor in what determines, subjectively of course, if a quarterback is any good. Interceptions also help determine if they aren’t any good.
Let’s look at those same six QBs, as compared to the national averages that season. We can also compare them against themselves.


