combined_graphic









So, we determined here that Caleb is good. Brady Olson still isn’t great. He actually wound up with more picks than scores. Ouch.
Henigan’s touchdown numbers went down a touch, but his interceptions didn’t go anywhere. Richardson threw less picks but had way less scores. Not exactly a shining star. The FBS average stayed about the same both seasons, around eight interceptions to 20 touchdowns.
Finn and Wolff saw some improvement. In Finn’s case, he was able to get his team into the end zone with the ball more. He also threw more than 70 additional passes his sophomore year, which led to the higher interception total. Wolff was the most drastic, though. His interceptions dropped and his touchdowns rose. Improvement! Old Dominion is on top of the world.
Okay, so there isn’t always noticeable improvement from Year 1 to Year 2. It is very dependent on the situation and the surrounding tools. NFL Draft QBs have to have done something right though? Right? Right?????

Lets look at the 2024 QB draft class, producing 10 different college products. They showed some growth from when they entered college football didn’t they? Generally speaking, yes.


Code
master_qb_list &lt;- bind_rows(qbs14, qbs15, qbs16, qbs17, qbs18, qbs19, qbs20, qbs21, qbs22, qbs23, qbs24, qbs25) |&gt; 
  group_by(Name) |&gt; 
  ungroup() |&gt; 
  arrange((Name))

first_draft_2024 &lt;- master_qb_list |&gt; 
  filter(Name == "Caleb Williams" | Name == "Jayden Daniels" | Name == "Drake Maye" | Name == "Michael Penix Jr." | Name == "J.J. McCarthy" | Name == "Bo Nix" | Name == "Spencer Rattler" | Name == "Jordan Travis" | Name == "Joe Milton" | Name == "Devin Leary" | Name == "Michael Pratt") |&gt; 
  mutate(
    Cl = ifelse(Name == "Jordan Travis" & Cl == "Sr." & `Pass TD` == 15, "Jr.", Cl),
    Cl = ifelse(Name == "Jordan Travis" & Cl == "Jr." & `Pass TD` == 24, "Sr.", Cl),
    Cl = ifelse(Name == "Jordan Travis" & Cl == "Sr." & `Pass TD` == 20, "Gr.", Cl),
    Cl = ifelse(Name == "Michael Pratt" & Cl == "Fr." & G == 12, "So.", Cl),
    Cl = ifelse(Name == "Jayden Daniels" & Cl == "Sr." & `Pass TD` == 40, "Gr.", Cl),
    Cl = ifelse(Name == "Bo Nix" & Cl == "Sr." & `Pass TD` == 45, "Gr.", Cl)
  ) |&gt; 
  add_row(Name = "Spencer Rattler", Cl = "So.", `Pass Att` = 187, `Pass Com` = 140) |&gt; 
  add_row(Name = "Devin Leary", Cl = "Jr.", `Pass Att` = 193, `Pass Com` = 118) |&gt; 
  arrange(desc(Name))

draft_2024 &lt;- first_draft_2024 |&gt; 
  mutate(PassingEff = `Pass Com`/`Pass Att`) |&gt;
  mutate(Cl = gsub("Fr.", "1", Cl, fixed=TRUE)) |&gt; 
  mutate(Cl = gsub("So.", "2", Cl, fixed=TRUE)) |&gt;
  mutate(Cl = gsub("Jr.", "3", Cl, fixed=TRUE)) |&gt;
  mutate(Cl = gsub("Sr.", "4", Cl, fixed=TRUE)) |&gt;
  mutate(Cl = gsub("Gr.", "5", Cl, fixed=TRUE)) |&gt; 
  mutate(Cl = as.numeric(as.character(Cl)))


ggplot() +
  geom_bump(data=draft_2024, aes(x = Cl, y = PassingEff, colour = Name, group = Name)) +
  geom_point(data=draft_2024, aes(x = Cl, y = PassingEff, colour = Name, group = Name), size = 2.5) +
  geom_text_repel(data = draft_2024 |&gt; group_by(Name) |&gt; filter(Cl == min(Cl)) |&gt; ungroup(), aes(x = Cl, y = PassingEff, label = Name), size = 3) +
  geom_text_repel(data = draft_2024 |&gt; group_by(Name) |&gt; filter(Cl == max(Cl)) |&gt; ungroup(), aes(x = Cl, y = PassingEff, label = Name), size = 3
  ) +
  scale_x_continuous(
    limits = c(0.5, 5.5),
    breaks = c(1, 2, 3, 4, 5)
  ) +
  scale_y_continuous(
    labels = percent_format(accuracy = 1)
  ) +
  scale_color_manual(values = c(
  "#1F77B4",
  "#FF7F0E",
  "#2CA02C",
  "#D62728",
  "#9467BD",
  "#8C564B",
  "#17BECF",
  "#E377C2",
  "#002d72",
  "#BCBD22",
  "#AEC7E8" 
  )) +
  labs(
    x = "Year in College Football",
    y = "Completion Percentage",
    title = "Career Completion Percentages for Quarterbacks Drafted in 2024",
    subtitle = "Graph does not include players who competed in less than 75% of their games unless it 
happened in the middle of career for continuity sake",
    caption = "Source: NCAA | By: Danny Berg"
  ) +
  theme_minimal(
  ) +
  theme(
    plot.title = element_text(size = 16, face = "bold"),
    axis.title = element_text(size = 8), 
    plot.subtitle = element_text(size=12), 
    panel.grid.minor = element_blank(),
    plot.title.position = "plot"
  )









With the exception of Devin Leary, a sixth-round compensation pick to the Baltimore Ravens, all of the signal-callers saw improvement from their first season to when they entered the college game. Exactly what an NFL team wants with a young guy entering the program.
Improvement year to year, especially for players on the younger side, is extremely unknown. It is hard to predict what a player as important as a quarterback is going to do in his sophomore, junior, and senior seasons based just off of one year. It is more difficult now than ever before with the transfer portal as a major player.
It is far from linear. Truly, the only thing that ultimately matters from the start to the end of one’s college football career is that you are in a better spot than where you left it. In most cases, that is exactly what happens.

A yearly occurrence in the National Football League, a handful of bottom-tier clubs will let their leader go in the middle of the season. Whether that is because they’re in the middle of a 14-game losing streak, are absolute morons, or might send an email or two, a few teams will look to shake up the locker room by firing the head coach.
Does it really do anything though? Are teams just trying to find a scapegoat and try and spiral out of control to a high draft pick? Or are things really just that desperate that anything could provide a spark? Continuity is always a puzzle, and disrupting it sometimes helps. Sometimes it doesn’t. It really depends.

But almost always, the firing of a head coach in the middle of the season means that the team has sucked. Bad.


